THE EXPERIENCED REALITY

Images · Sound · Language · Emotion · Narrative · Interaction

The crucial thing is that changing our interpretation doesn’t necessarily require changing the underlying event.

One simulated encounter could give rise to a hundred genuinely different experiences.

Same event. Different worlds of meaning.

Imagine a gladiator standing in a Roman arena as his opponent approaches.

Change the lens

One underlying scenario

HistoricalCinematicPoeticMythologicalFantasyScientific

Historical perspective

The arena is hot. His equipment constrains his movement. He knows the customs of his profession, but not the verdict of the crowd. We see the event through the material and social realities of his time.

That’s just the beginning, because we could mix lenses rather than choosing only one.

A historically grounded, cinematographically dramatic, poetically narrated, first-person account is a perfectly legitimate combination.

But a fantasy lens that adds magic to the underlying world is a different operation: it changes the simulation itself, not merely our presentation of it. The system should preserve that distinction.

